00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.950 align:start position:0%
 
recently i've been getting the  impression that there might be this  slight misconception online that i am  somehow highly organized and have my  life together and i just want to say  that this cannot be farther from the  truth when i am being generous with  myself i call myself an artistic spirit  and when i'm being realistic i call  myself a chaotic mess which is what my  life looks like most of the time one  thing that i struggle with a lot is  habit formation i don't like habits i  don't like sticking to them i don't like  creating them i don't like having them  it's just very very hard for me to keep  doing things regularly and so of course  i had to go to the scientific literature  to understand what really creates habits  and what are the good and bad things how  can i be somehow sabotaging potentially  myself in my habit creation and  improving my life and because it is the  season in january to kind of get  ourselves together and start to  establish new routines and schedules i  thought what better time to kind of  share what i found with you so in this  video i'm going to go through firstly  two things that we might be doing  already which i think are kind of like  general advice which is very common  around habit formation which actually  might be sabotaging the formation of  your habits so  general advice that is not good and then  on the flip side also some other things  that we could be doing instead in order  to create better habits so let's get  started the first thing that we might  instinctively think is good but is  actually bad for our habits is creating  very strict schedules around when to do  things so writing to-do lists such as  i'm going to wake up at 8am and then i'm  going to go to the gym until 9 and then  i'm going to do 400 past med questions  and then i'm going to write 3 000 words  of my essay and run a half marathon  these sort of scheduling of habits tend  to not work in the long term and this  has definitely been the case for me i've  very ambitiously created these very  strict routines for my life and i can  tell you right now they never last a  long time they are never able to  healthily stick to these for weeks let  alone months or years potentially and if  you're the kind of person who says well  this actually works very well for me are  these habits that you enjoy doing for  very very long times or are these things  that you forced yourself to do  temporarily because this tends to be the  difference between long-term habits and  short-term habits so it might be that  just scheduling things in very in a very  strict to-do list kind of way is  actually counterproductive towards  establishing habits that you find  enjoyable the second thing which i quite  enjoy to hear is that putting  notifications on your phone for example  or electronic devices is a bad way to  establish habits we tend to in the long  term just ignore these notifications and  we don't internalize the process of  doing something again and again so they  tend to be a bad way to establish a  long-term habit they might work  temporarily to remind you to do  something but they will do very little  for internalizing a behavior  consistently for the future okay great  so these are two things that we can  ignore doing what should we be doing  instead the first thing is quite  interesting and fun and that is putting  yourself through a mental exercise of  doing an activity consistently before or  instead of actually doing it so for  example let's say i want to establish a  habit of going to the gym because i'm  just not doing this what would be a good  thing for me to do is to just close my  eyes and imagine every single step in as  much detail as possible that would take  me from what i'm doing right now to  being in the gym so for example i will  get off this couch right now and i'm  going to go to my closet and i'm going  to get my gym clothes and put my gym  gear on i'm going to take my bag and my  face mask and very importantly my  airpods and my phone and my keys i'm  going to walk out my door go to my  elevator so every single detailed step  hooking ourselves through this will  reinforce the neural pathways in our  brain which are responsible for us  actually doing these activities so  through role-playing these activities  very safely in our minds we are actually  reducing the activation energy that is  necessary for physically doing these  activities in the future so something  which is very easy to do is that in your  free time if for example you have  decided that you're going to  procrastinate tonight and you're just  not going to write that essay you're  just not going to go to the gym what you  can do instead which would still be a  productive thing is just to close your  eyes and imagine you actually doing that  activity because it will make it much  more likely apparently that you can do  it with a lot less friction eventually  in the future and establish this habit  in a stronger way second thing has to do  with timing around habits and it kind of  ties into what i mentioned earlier in  that very strictly planning and  scheduling when you're going to do  things is a bad way to establish a  long-term habit the dorsolateral  striatum is what becomes activated  before and after we do a task so when we  are performing a task or establishing a  habit we are kind of bracketing this  around the activation of the  dorsolateral striatum so we are  bracketing around what we do before and  what we do after and so the more  established the habit the weaker this  connection so any sort of activity that  we want to do we want to make it as  loosely associated as possible with  everything around it because imagine if  we had these very strict schedules of  i'm only going to do b if a happens and  then i'm only going to do c if b happens  it's very unlikely that these things  won't become completely chaotic at some  point if i only go to the gym after i've  had my very special breakfast smoothie  what happens on the days that i don't  have the ingredients for my smoothie and  if i'm only going to do my past med  exercises on the days that i have had a  good workout what happens when i don't  do a good workout and just making these  things so dependent on one another and  what happens before and after them means  that we're much less likely to create a  habit and our strongest habits are those  that are as most context independent as  possible which basically means are those  activities that we can do no matter what  else is happening around us for example  brushing your teeth tends to be a very  context independent activity because for  most of us no matter how tired we are no  matter what time of the day we are going  to sleep no matter when we are supposed  to wake up the next morning no matter  what kind of mood we are in no matter if  we've had or haven't had dinner for most  of us we will brush our teeth no matter  what this is a very context independent  activity while tying things into a to-do  list or into a strict schedule means  that we will be very context-dependent  in the habits that we are creating and  therefore those habits tend to be weaker  having said this very interestingly  there is another way to kind of plan  when you are supposed to do things that  is not dependent on what you have done  in the day but is dependent on the  phasing or time of the day i found this  way of viewing habits very very  interesting when you divide your day  into three phases phase one is the first  eight hours after you wake up  irrespective when you wake up you might  be at 8am person or you might be a 4am  person it doesn't matter the second  phase is the 8 to 16 hours after you  wake up and the third phase is the 16 to  24 hours after you wake up the first  eight hours are those wherein you have  the most energy to do things that you  don't want to do so this is where you  put the activities that you really don't  enjoy and the new habits that you are  creating that take a lot of physical or  mental energy or physical or mental  barriers to get over if you despise  going to the gym this might be the time  to do it if you despise studying or  doing questions or doing a project or  doing work this is the time to do it in  the second phase of the day this is  where you do the activities that do take  mental and physical energy but you find  a bit more enjoyable this is why i  realize i am a evening gym person i'm  not a morning gym person and i find it  very easy and enjoyable to go to the gym  in the evening because even though it  takes a lot of physical energy it  doesn't take a lot of mental energy for  me because i actually want to do it but  if you would hate the gym it would be  good to do that in the morning on the  opposite side doing things such as like  studying sometimes that's not my  favorite thing to do so it's not good  for me to leave that as the last thing  in the day but it's better for me to do  that in phase one of the day and in  phase three where we should be sleeping  this is where we put the habits which  take very little physical or emotional  energy so these are things like oh i  want to start a journaling habit or i  want to read a bit more or i want to  call my parents this is a good time to  establish these habits because  these are things that we very much want  to do but are not too tiring unless of  course you find feeding very very  difficult in which case you might want  to upgrade it to a phase two or a phase  one of the day so kind of seeing the day  in this way where in if you want to  establish the habit you ask yourself hmm  how much activation energy does this  have is this something that i really  enjoy doing is this something that i  could do when i'm tired or do i want to  do it first thing in the morning kind of  like swallow the frog and slutting it  there and also moving it around so  sometimes doing it in hour one sometimes  doing it in hour seven sometimes doing  it in hour three or phase one will make  it very context independent and very  likely that you can repeat this activity  again and again so yes going down the  pathway of scientific exploration and  studies that i go through for these  videos kind of puts me in this mindset  of wanting to learn more about science  and understanding how things work and  one thing that i've been using over the  last few months to kind of supplement my  scientific literacy is using brilliant  which are very kindly sponsoring this  video the thing that i like about  brilliant is that i can use puzzles and  games so i can definitely do this as a  phase three activity before i go to  sleep and i end up surprisingly learning  things where i think that oh why was  this never explained this way for me in  high school or university i'm currently  working through the statistic courses  because they tend to be my least  favorite part of maths when i was in  school but unfortunately kind of the  most one of the most important parts of  maths if you are approaching scientific  literature i'm hopefully becoming a bit  more interested in this sort of  mathematical thinking if you would like  to join me on this phase the activity  there will be a link in my description  where you can try out brilliant and get  a discount on the yearly subscription  otherwise if you made it to the end of  this video thank you so much for  spending this time with me i hope you  have a wonderful rest of your day be  kind yourself and others and don't  believe everything you think thanks bye

